<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-21059] tests: stack_trap in a test chains the script EXIT trap</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21059</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Problem"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Problem&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A &lt;tt&gt;stack_trap ... EXIT&lt;/tt&gt; call in a test also runs the EXIT trap of the test script when the test ends. The script trap then runs once for each test that calls &lt;tt&gt;stack_trap&lt;/tt&gt;, and again when the script exits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In sanity-lnet.sh, the script trap is &lt;tt&gt;cleanup_testsuite&lt;/tt&gt;. If the suite starts with Lustre mounted, &lt;tt&gt;restore_mounts&lt;/tt&gt; is true, and &lt;tt&gt;cleanup_testsuite&lt;/tt&gt; calls &lt;tt&gt;setupall&lt;/tt&gt; in the middle of the suite. The later tests then find Lustre mounted and fail. For this reason, no sanity-lnet test uses &lt;tt&gt;stack_trap&lt;/tt&gt; today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Environment"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Environment&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;master at 2.17.60&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;bash 5.1.8 (EL9) and bash 5.3. Bash 3.2 does not show the problem.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Reproduction"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Reproduction&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Mount Lustre.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Add a sanity-lnet test that calls &lt;tt&gt;stack_trap "echo cleanup" EXIT&lt;/tt&gt; and prints &lt;tt&gt;trap -p EXIT&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Run the test with ONLY.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;


&lt;p&gt;The test prints:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="preformatted panel" style="border-width: 1px;"&gt;&lt;div class="preformattedContent panelContent"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;trap -- 'echo test_30 cleanup; cleanup_testsuite' EXIT
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After the test, the log shows "Starting client" from &lt;tt&gt;setupall&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Analysis"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Analysis&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;tt&gt;run_one_logged&lt;/tt&gt; runs each test in a subshell. The subshell does not run the EXIT trap of the script. But &lt;tt&gt;trap -p&lt;/tt&gt; in the subshell still prints that trap. &lt;tt&gt;stack_trap&lt;/tt&gt; reads the &lt;tt&gt;trap -p&lt;/tt&gt; output and adds the script trap to the test trap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Other suites with a script-level EXIT trap have the same problem:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;large-lun.sh sets &lt;tt&gt;trap cleanupall EXIT&lt;/tt&gt;. test_2 and test_5 call &lt;tt&gt;stack_trap&lt;/tt&gt;, so each of them runs &lt;tt&gt;cleanupall&lt;/tt&gt; when it ends.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;sanity-ec.sh sets &lt;tt&gt;stack_trap "unset LFS_EC_OK" EXIT&lt;/tt&gt;. Each sanity-ec test that calls &lt;tt&gt;stack_trap&lt;/tt&gt; runs that command again. The effect is harmless.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;sanity.sh sets no script-level EXIT trap, so sanity.sh does not show the problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Proposedfix"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Proposed fix&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Clear the EXIT trap at the start of &lt;tt&gt;run_one&lt;/tt&gt; with &lt;tt&gt;trap - EXIT&lt;/tt&gt;. Add a sanity-lnet test that checks that a test EXIT trap holds only the test commands.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
